===Monarchy and royal family===
{{Main|House of Zhifol}}

Qym is an absolute monarchy controlled by the [[House of Zhifol]], led by the [[King of Qym]], currently [[Qodzar of Qym|Qodzar]] since {{abbr|2443|2029.46 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. The [[Fundamental Law of Qym]] is a constitution-like charter defining the responsibilities and duties of government institutions. It was adopted by King [[Bacha XII]] in {{abbr|2123|1822 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} to modernize the country during the [[Twelfth Era Reforms]].

===Government===
{{Main|Government of Qym}}

The King appoints the Royal Ministers, who form the government. Royal Ministers must be members of the royal family. They are political appointees and administratively operate their ministries. They are executives with full administrative control over their respective ministries. Under Qym's political system, the terms government and Government of Qym refer specifically to the King and the Royal Ministers, but typically also includes the national public service, and ministries and agencies when used elsewhere. It excludes regional and municipal governments who are referred by their respective administrative names, even though most of them are also directly appointed by the King. Ministers each have their own staff, called a ministerial cabinet. Each cabinet consists of around ten to twenty members, who are political appointees. Cabinet members assist the minister in running a ministry. Members of ministerial cabinets are powerful figures within the government and work in both the political and administrative spheres. The hierarchy in each ministerial cabinet is determined by the minister. Working groups consisting of representatives from several ministries are commonplace. It is the duty of the King to oversee these inter-ministry meetings and to ensure that government work is done effectively and efficiently. All ministerial cabinet decisions must be co-signed by the King.

===Legislature===
{{Main|Royal Consultative Assembly}}

Qym's unicameral legislature is the [[Royal Consultative Assembly]]. It is a deliberative assembly that advises the King on issues that are important to the kingdom. It has the power to propose laws to the King of Qym and his cabinet to approve and pass. It has 100 members, all appointed by the king and dismissible if he chooses to. Since {{abbr|2374|1972 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, the Assembly requires a 40 percent minimum quota for women by royal decree. It also comprises ten specialized commissions with ten members each. The Assembly has the power to draft bills for the cabinet or the King, propose revisions and amendments, and request audiences with ministers who are not members of the royal family. The Assembly is authorized to propose bills and transmit them to the King, but only he has the power to approve and enforce them. It conducts collegial studies of the annual reports prepared by ministries and official bodies. Its advice is transmitted to the King to help him decide on policy decisions, international treaties, and economic plans. Finally, it can examine the annual budgets and request information from the various ministries. 

===Law===
{{Main|Law of Qym|Law enforcement in Qym}}

Qym uses a hybrid civil law system based on the [[Fundamental Law of Qym]], adopted by King [[Bacha XII]] in {{abbr|2123|1822 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, which acts as a ''de facto'' constitution. The Fundamental Law places restrictions on preceding and subsequent royal decrees to comply to it. The Fundamental Law states that "provisions that contradict those of higher rank are invalid." This means that there is a hierarchy of norms that distinguishes between lower-ranking and higher-ranking norms. However, the relationship between norms is not limited to a question of hierarchy but also involves a question of jurisdiction. The hierarchy of norms in Qymish law is as follows:

*The Fundamental Law of {{abbr|2123|1822 (Earth Year Equivalent)}},
*Public international law (treaties, conventions, and agreements),
*law (in the strict sense): organic laws, ordinary laws, and regulatory norms having the rank of law (royal decree-law and royal legislative decree),
*norms emanating from the executive branch within the hierarchy of the bodies that promulgate them: royal decrees, ministerial decrees and orders, etc.

The judiciary comprises the Council of the Judiciary, the administrative and supervisory body for judges and magistrates, as well as personnel exercising legal authority in Qym; and the [[Supreme Court of Qym|Supreme Court]], which oversees the Qymish legal system, including upholding the Fundamental Law, and acts as the court of last resort for crimes and offenses, and as the court of first instance for certain serious crimes and offenses. A unique feature of the Qymish legal system is the [[Royal Court of Qym|Royal Court]], a tribunal that tries Qymish citizens and foreigners for crimes and offenses of an international nature or for specific issues that may involve third-party states, as well as current events such as terrorist acts, and attacks against the Crown and its representatives, in other words the royal family. 

The jurisprudence of the [[Supreme Court of Qym|Supreme Court]] is a complementary source for the interpretation and application of the law. The Supreme Court decides on the legality of a decision but also verifies whether the decisions of lower courts are consistent with jurisprudence. Due to the principle of [[Royal immunity in Qym|royal immunity]], present in the Fundamental Law, members of the royal family are exempt from ordinary courts and are tried only before the Supreme Court. Only the King retains sovereign immunity, as all laws and decisions are made in his name, meaning that he has absolute immunity from criminal prosecution. There is no legal pathway for abdication in Qymish law besides the age requirement of a reigning monarch to be between {{abbr|30|25 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and {{abbr|75|62 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} years of age. In practice, this means a monarch must minimally be {{abbr|30|25 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} years old to be crowned and automatically abdicates the crown upon turning {{abbr|75|62 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, as it passes on to the king's successor. 

===Administrative divisions===
Qym is divided into 5 [[wikipedia:duchy|duchies]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⰲⱖⱝⱁⰳⰾⰺⰽ, ''Vühoglyk''), 10 [[wikipedia:county|counties]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⰳⰰⱇⰺⱄⱅⱁ, ''Gafysto''), and 5 [[wikipedia:march (territory)|marches]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⱂⰰⰽⱃⰰⱋ, ''Pakrash''). These first-level administrative divisions are headed by a [[wikipedia:duke|duke or duchess]], a [[wikipedia:count|count or countess]], or a [[wikipedia:marquess|marquess or marchioness]], respectively, who are all appointed by the [[King of Qym]] and act as his local representatives. While their titles are different in name and prestige, in practice they all have equal powers over their respective administrative division. These divisions are further divided into [[wikipedia:Barony (county division)|baronies]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: Ⰿⰰⱜⰵⱋ, ''Majesh''), which are headed by a [[wikipedia:baron|baron or baroness]], usually appointed by the king's representatives. 

