===Foreign Relations===
{{Main|Foreign relations of Qym}}

Qym belongs to several international organizations, including the [[Basis Forum]], as a founding member during the [[Boreal War]]. It is also a founding member and permanent host of the [[Boreal Cooperation Council]], which was established following that war. Among other prominent international organizations, Qym is a member of the [[International Trade Federation]] and the [[Basis Climatic Organization]]. It also hosts the headquarters of the [[Basis Development Bank]] and the [[Organization of Sukatic Nations]]. At the continental level, Qym is part of the [[Meranian Diplomatic League]], the primary intergovernmental organization in [[Meran]]. 

Qymish foreign policy after the [[Boreal War]] has been primarily shaped by the expiration of the [[Nagur Pact]] and geopolitical tension with former ally [[WILLEMIA]] despite deep trade ties (?). On the other hand, Qym developed close ties with former adversaries [[NEIGHBOR2]] and [[NEIGHBOR3]], both economically and militarily. Since {{abbr|2340|1944 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym has been one of the main proponents of political [[wikipedia:multilateralism|multilateralism]], [[wikipedia:economic globalization|economic globalization]] and [[wikipedia:free trade|free trade]]. It is party to the most [[Free trade agreements of Qym|free trade agreements]] of any country on Basis and is currently negotiating several more agreements as of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. 

[[List of diplomatic missions of Qym|Qym has diplomatic and consular offices]] in over ### locations in approximately ### foreign countries. Qym also hosts over ### diplomatic missions, including ### embassies in [[Zhol]]'s [[Embassy Quarter (Zhol)|Embassy Quarter]]. [[NEIGHBOR]] is the country with the most diplomatic missions in Qym, having one embassy and # consulates. 

===Military===
[[File:RAFQ Ramiq R-20.jpg|250px|thumb|right|A [[Royal Air Force of Qym|RAFQ]] [[Ramiq]] [[Ramiq R-20|R-20]] fighter jet.]]{{Main|Qymish Armed Forces}}

The [[Royal Army of Qym|Royal Army]], [[Royal Navy of Qym|Royal Navy]], and [[Royal Air Force of Qym|Royal Air Force]] are collectively known as the [[Qymish Armed Forces]]. All three branches are administered by the [[Royal Ministry of Defense (Qym)|Royal Ministry of Defense]], while the [[wikipedia:Commander-in-Chief|Commander-in-Chief]] is the [[King of Qym]], to whom members of the forces swear an oath of allegiance. With 561,434 active personnel, Qym maintains [[List of countries on Basis by number of military and paramilitary personnel|one of the largest armed forces on Basis]]. 

As of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, total defense expenditure represents $251.9 billion or 3.1% of GDP in nominal terms, the [[List of countries on Basis by military expenditures|?th largest on Basis]]. However, on a purchasing power parity basis, Qym's {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} military budget was the equivalent of $295.8 billion. The Qymish military-industrial sector is significant, with major state-owned companies [[Ramiq]] and [[Vajdila]] having a leading role in the [[Aerospace industry in Qym|aerospace sector]] in particular. The country has produced high quality military equipment such as the [[Ramiq R-26]] and [[Vajdila V-33]] fighters, the [[BQ (missile)|BQ]] missile, the [[Vezhpaqa]] main battle tank, and the [[King Bacha-class aircraft carrier]], among others, resulting in considerable arms exports, although [[Ramiq]] products are prohibited from being exported.

===Human rights===
{{Main|Human rights in Qym}}

The [[Fundamental Law of Qym|Fundamental Law]] states that fundamental rights of citizens include freedom of speech, freedom of the press, the right to a fair trial, freedom of religion, and the right to property. However, in practice, the law does not provide meaningful protection against criminal prosecution by the state, particularly through the [[Royal Court of Qym|Royal Court]]. Censorship of political speech and information is often used to silence criticism of the government and the royal family. Security forces allegedly commit human rights abuses such as torture, and the judiciary does not always prosecute or investigate them. Unauthorized street demonstrations or protests, even peaceful ones, are generally repressed. Press activity is regulated by the [[Professional Code of Qym]] and overseen by the [[Qymish Order of Journalists]], the [[wikipedia:Professional order|professional order]] for journalists. In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym ranked ###th out of ### countries in [[Journalistic Liberty Organization]]'s [[Basis Press Freedom Index]]. 

Long-standing human rights issues include the [[wikipedia:absolute monarchy|absence of political representation]] and [[wikipedia:responsible government|responsible government]], the treatment of [[Ethnic groups in Qym|ethnic]] and religious minorities, [[Immigration to Qym|immigrants]], [[wikipedia:police brutality|police brutality]] and [[wikipedia:racial|ethnic profiling]], and [[LGBTQ rights in Qym|discrimination and violence against LGBTQ people]]. Other contemporary issues include [[wikipedia:mass surveillance|mass surveillance]], infringements on the [[wikipedia:right to privacy|right to privacy]], [[wikipedia:digital rights|digital rights]] and the [[wikipedia:Right to repair|right to repair]], and [[wikipedia:copyright|copyright]] issues pertaining to [[wikipedia:Generative AI|generative AI]] and [[wikipedia:copyright infringement|piracy]].

==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Qym}}

Qym has a highly developed [[wikipedia:dirigisme|dirigist]] [[wikipedia:mixed economy|mixed economy]] which has also been described as [[wikipedia:state capitalism|state capitalism]] or as "one big national [[wikipedia:zaibatsu|zaibatsu]] or [[wikipedia:chaebol|chaebol]]" by economist [[FOREIGNER]]. It has a labor force of roughly 90 million<!--comparable to Canada in terms of % of total pop-->, which is the [[List of countries on Basis by size of the labor force|second largest on Basis]]. 

Major economic sectors of competitive strength include public utilities, energy generation, manufacturing, [[Aerospace industry in Qym|aerospace]], [[Automotive industry in Qym|automobiles]], rail, steel, minerals, environmental services, banking, insurance, pharmaceuticals, IT services, telecommunications, e-commerce, and real estate. Most of these sectors are dominated by [[List of crown corporations of Qym|Qym's numerous crown corporations]], many of which being global leaders and enjoying a domestic monopoly. 

In terms of international trade, Qym is both the [[List of countries by exports|largest exporter]] and [[List of countries by imports|largest importer]] on Basis as of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. [[Thyon]] is its largest trading partner, accounting for 12.7% of the total trade. Other large trading partners include [[NEIGHBOR1]], [[NEIGHBOR2]], [[NEIGHBOR3]], [[NEIGHBOR4]], and [[NEIGHBOR5]]. Qym's main exports are automobiles, machinery, pharmaceuticals, aircraft, minerals, electricity, professional services, and electronics. Meanwhile, its main imports are machinery, electronics, oil and gas, minerals, automobiles, agricultural products, tourism, and textiles and clothing. 

===Energy===
[[File:Nuclear.power.plant.Dukovany.jpg|250px|thumb|right|A nuclear power station in Qym. The country produces around 42.6% of its electricity from nuclear power.]]{{Main|Energy in Qym}}

In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym was the [[List of countries on Basis by electricity consumption|largest consumer of energy on Basis]]. It was also the [[List of countries on Basis by electricity production|largest producer of electricity]] that same year. [[Apshvjespa]], a [[wikipedia:crown corporation|crown corporation]], is tasked with producing and distributing electricity and is one of the largest electric utility company on Basis. The country consumed 2,510 TWh of electricity in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} (15.46 MWh per capita) and produced 2,808 TWh. As of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, 1,069.26 TWh (42.6%) of electricity in Qym is produced from nuclear, 479.41 TWh (19.1%) from hydropower, 318.77 TWh (12.7%) from wind, 288.65 TWh (11.5%) from natural gas, 208.33 TWh (8.3%) from solar and 145.58 TWh (5.8%) from biomass and waste, among other sources. [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|King Bacha XVIII]] has committed to phase out its natural gas plants by the end of the existing plants' lifetime.

===Transport===
{{Main|Transport in Qym}}

Qym has a large road network, with a total length of ###,### km in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, with most of it paved. Driving is done [[wikipedia:Left- and right-hand traffic|on the right]]. The Qymish highway system is extensive and connects all major cities, combining for a total of NUM expressways, all of which are tolled and numbered with even numbers for north-south routes and odd ones for east-west routes. [[wikipedia:Ring road|Ring road]] expressways are an exception to this rule, as they feature a zero (0) in front of their number to indicate it is a circling route. Toll fares are collected electronically nationwide since {{abbr|2392|1987 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. Speed limits on highways are fixed at {{abbr|300 km/h|136.36 km/h (Earth Speed Equivalent)}}<!--Speed per hour is essentially 2.2 times that of Earth for an hour length--> in rural areas and {{abbr|250 km/h|113.63 km/h (Earth Speed Equivalent)}} in urban areas. On rural roads, the speed limit is {{abbr|150 km/h|68.18 km/h (Earth Speed Equivalent)}}, while on city roads such as boulevards, avenues, or streets, the speed limit is {{abbr|100 km/h|45.45 km/h (Earth Speed Equivalent)}}. In school zones, the speed limit is reduced to {{abbr|70 km/h|31.81 km/h (Earth Speed Equivalent)}}. 
[[File:Luftaufnahme BMW Group Werk Dingolfing, Cropped and Straightened.jpg|250px|thumb|left|A [[Tarez]] plant in [[Chelis]].]]
In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, there were 83,615,184 registered motor vehicles, making Qym the [[List of countries and territories on Basis by motor vehicles per capita|third largest automotive market on Basis]]. It is also the [[List of countries on Basis by motor vehicle production|second largest producer of motor vehicles]] at around 7 million units per year, with [[Cajech]], [[Marnyj]] and [[Tarez]] being its main automakers. Those three also produce a large number of vehicles outside of Qym, with many factories around [[Basis]]. Since {{abbr|2430|2019 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, 100% of new car sales are [[wikipedia:electric vehicle|fully electric vehicles]], being one of the first countries to reach a full EV market share. [[wikipedia:Internal combustion engine|Internal combustion engine]] vehicles remain available through the resale market, but new cars must be EVs per Qymish law. [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|King Bacha XVIII]] promulgated several new automotive regulations to increase safety during his reign, notably [[wikipedia:Intelligent speed assistance|GPS-activated speed limiters]] and manufacturing visibility standards to mitigate [[wikipedia:vehicle blind spot|vehicular blind spots]]. In {{abbr|2442|2028.6 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, [[Bacha XX of Qym|King Bacha XX]] introduced a decree mandating manufacturing changes to newer car models, requiring them to have physical switches or buttons for primary driving components, such as engine start/shut, door handles, door locks, gear selection, turn signals, and hazard lights. Restrictions on [[wikipedia:In-car entertainment|in-car entertainment]] systems have also been put in place to temper [[wikipedia:distracted driving|distracted driving]]. 
[[File:Etr500.JPG|250px|thumb|right|A high-speed train from [[Qymaja Gelis]].]]
[[Rail transport in Qym]], operated by [[Qymaja Gelis]]<!--Qymaja Gelis; Qymish Rail--> (QG), a [[wikipedia:crown corporation|crown corporation]], is among [[List of countries on Basis by rail usage|the busiest on Basis]] for both passengers and freight. As of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, the country had ###,### km of railways, the ?th-longest network globally. International passenger rail flows through four recognized [[wikipedia:Port of entry|stations of entry]] where passengers need to get off and go through customs before entering or leaving the country. These are [[TBD]] to the west, [[TBD]] to the east, [[TBD]] to the southwest or southeast, and [[Teurek]] to the southwest or southeast. Additional stations of entry are planned based on new high-speed rail lines. 

As of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, 7 Qymish metropolitan areas have [[wikipedia:rapid transit|high-capacity metro systems]], namely [[Zhol Metro|Zhol]], [[Nagur Metro|Nagur]], [[Patsalys Metro|Patsalys]], [[Byvichek Metro|Byvichek]], [[Teurek Metro|Teurek]], [[Leramy Metro|Leramy]] and [[Kalnuj Metro|Kalnuj]]. A further 18 cities have [[wikipedia:medium-capacity rail system|medium-capacity metro systems]] or [[wikipedia:semi-metro|semi-metro systems]] with full [[wikipedia:grade separation|grade separation]]. Rail systems without grade separation have been illegal in Qymish urban areas since the country dismantled its tramway networks and replaced them by buses in the 1950s during the reign of [[Bacha XVI of Qym|King Bacha XVI]]. State-owned [[ShMÃ]] (ⰛⰏⰫ)<!--Shkoji Mözdoñy Ãsarhë; Royal Locomotive Company--> is one of the [[Big 4 (rolling stock)|big 4]] global [[wikipedia:rolling stock|rolling stock]] manufacturers and provides trains for most of the country's systems. 

There are 955 airports in Qym, of which 661 are paved and 294 are unpaved. [[King Bacha XV International Airport]] is the [[List of the busiest airports in Qym|largest and busiest airport in the country]], handling the vast majority of popular and commercial traffic and connecting [[Zhol]] with virtually all major cities across the world. [[QMR]] is the national carrier airline and legally the sole domestic provider, although numerous private airline companies provide international travel services. 

The [[port of Nagur]] is by far the busiest port in the country and on [[Basis]], handling around 45% of all incoming and outgoing maritime trade to Qym. 

===Science and technology===
{{Main|Science and technology in Qym}}[[File:Стартовый комплекс космодрома Восточный перед первым пуском.jpg|250px|thumb|right|The [[Umach spacesport]] before a launch.]]

Representing 4.63% of its GDP in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym is one of the [[List of countries on Basis by research and development spending|biggest spender in research and development globally]]. Most of that research is in fields which Qymish [[wikipedia:crown corporation|crown corporations]] excel in, namely [[Aerospace industry in Qym|aerospace]], [[wikipedia:energy|energy]], [[wikipedia:pharmaceutical industry|pharmaceuticals]] and [[wikipedia:information technology|information technology]]. 

====Space====
{{Main|Qymish space program}}

The [[Qymish space program]] started in {{abbr|2348|1950.5 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. Qym launched its first satellite, [[Tygaj]], on DAY MONTH {{abbr|2358|1959 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, aboard a [[Dalica (rocket family)|Dalica-1]] rocket, making it the ?th country to place a satellite in orbit. With space launch capability provided by the [[Dalica (rocket family)|Dalica rocket family]] and two spaceports ([[Tajmus spaceport|Tajmus]] and [[Umach spaceport|Umach]]), Qym conducts the ?th highest number of orbital launches each year. 

====Telecommunications====
{{Main|Telecommunications in Qym}}

Qym is one of the largest telecom market on Basis and currently has the [[List of countries on Basis by number of mobile numbers in use|?th-largest number of active cellphones of any country]], with over 220 million subscribers, as of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. [[Tatiqym]], a [[wikipedia:crown corporation|crown corporation]], has a monopoly on providing telecommunication services, including fixed line, mobile telephony, internet services, and digital television. Considered a public service, telecommunication is affordable and widespread in Qym, although human rights groups have expressed concerns over government surveillance and censorship.

