Qym has a population of 162.36 million<!--162,359,580--> as of the {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} census, making it the [[List of countries on Basis by population|?th most populous country on Basis]]. The population grew at a stable annual rate of 0.575391% between {{abbr|2400|1994 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. 

Qym's first census in {{abbr|2200|1827.6 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} recorded a population of 28.72 million, reaching 50 million in {{abbr|2300|1910.7 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, 100 million in {{abbr|2360|1960.51 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, 150 million in {{abbr|2425|2014.51 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, and projections estimate it will grow to 200 million by {{abbr|2480|2060 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. In the last {{abbr|200 years|166 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, from {{abbr|2240|1861 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, the population was multiplied fivefold. Qym saw slow population growth from {{abbr|2330|1935.6 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|2440|1943.9 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} due to mass casualties during the [[Boreal War]]. 

High fertility rates persisted until the {{abbr|2370s|1968.8 to 1977 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, after which they started to decline; the [[wikipedia:total fertility rate|total fertility rate]] (TFR) reached an all-time low of 1.51 children per woman in {{abbr|2393|1988 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, well below the replacement rate of 2.1. In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, it stood at 1.56. 

Population density is estimated at NUM per square kilometer (NUM/sq mi) in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. 

===Urbanization===
{{Main|Urbanization in Qym}}[[File:Zhol_triplexes.jpg|250px|thumb|left|Wall-to-wall triplexes in [[Zhol]], with characteristic exterior staircases and balconies common in most Qymish cities.]]

Qym is highly urbanized, with around 75% of the population living in cities. The most densely populated parts of the country are the [[Foldava]] and [[Mizhpy]] river valleys, who intersect at the capital city of [[Zhol]], near the geographical center of the country. As of the {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} census, 11 metropolitan areas had more than 1 million residents. The largest of these are [[Zhol]], [[Nagur]] and [[Patsalys]] — all three located along the [[Foldava]] — with 21.1, 8.4, and 6.2 million inhabitants respectively. Nagur is located to the south of Zhol, while Patsalys is located to the northwest. [[Byvichek]], the 4th largest metropolitan area, also has a significant population of 4.6 million inhabitants and is located along the [[Mizhpy]] river to the northeast of Zhol. [[Teurek]], the 5th largest with a population 3.1 million, is part of the [[NAME]] conurbation with neighboring [[CITY1]], [[COUNTRY1]] and [[CITY2]], [[COUNTRY2]], which has a total population of ~10 million. The Zhol metropolitan area accounts for 13% of the total population of Qym, while the [[Duchy of Zhol]] accounts for 21%. 

Throughout history and even in modern times, Qymish cities have been highly influenced by the [[List of rulers of Qym|ruling king]] and their personal urbanist preferences. Most kings took into account the expertise of urban planners of their times, while some did not. No city has been more affected by the "whims of kings" than Zhol, the capital and largest city. In the [[Fourteenth Bachan era]] under [[Bacha XIV of Qym|Bacha XIV]], Qym experienced a growing rate of urbanization with people moving from rural areas to cities seeking employment. To house these people, often lower class industrial workers, wall-to-wall duplex and triplex dwellings were built at industrial scales by the [[Royal Housing Commission (Qym)|Royal Housing Commission]] initially in Zhol and then throughout the country. These working-class neighborhoods were often located near railyards or waterways, such as canals or rivers, in proximity to the workers' workplaces. More of these neighborhoods were built during the [[Fifteenth Bachan era]] that immediately followed under [[Bacha XV of Qym|Bacha XV]]. These plexes have become emblematic of Qymish housing and are a feature of any Qymish city. 

From the {{abbr|2340s|1944 to 1952.2 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} onward, the Qymish middle class tended to move further away from city centers into suburbs with single-family homes, accelerating the phenomenon of [[wikipedia:urban sprawl|urban sprawl]] throughout Qym. During the {{abbr|2390s|1985.4 to 1993.7 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, many of the old working-class neighborhoods became gentrified as industries relocated further away from the urban core to reduce pollution. Replacing the former industrial areas were larger apartment buildings, often catering to higher income people, while low income people displaced by gentrification moved to working-class suburbs, often comprising pre-fabricated dwellings close to new industrial areas. During the [[Eighteenth Bachan era]] under [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|Bacha XVIII]], from {{abbr|2409|2001 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, significant changes were made to urban planning, including environmental regulations and the sustainable development of cities. In most cities, traffic reduction measures were put in place, including bike paths, speed limits and speed bumps, bollards, slow zones, congestion pricing, and the pedestrianization of certain streets or sectors, particularly in commercial zones and historic centers. For instance, the [[Old City of Zhol]] became seasonally pedestrian starting  in {{abbr|2415|2006 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and pedestrian throughout the year since {{abbr|2422|2012 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. Moreover, many highways were transformed into urban boulevards, while parking lots were developed for housing or commercial use. 

Around 55% of Qymish people are homeowners, while the remaining 45% are renters. Most renters live in housing units built and maintained by the [[Royal Housing Commission (Qym)|Royal Housing Commission]] and are effectively paying rent to the Crown. The homeownership rate is much lower in major cities while it is much higher in rural areas. The [[Royal Housing Commission (Qym)|Royal Housing Commission]] also keeps rents stables by establishing benchmarks to private landlords on rent increases. It also has the mission to ensure [[wikipedia:housing starts|housing starts]] are stable and consistent with population growth, as well as demand for specific dwelling-types. 

