===Ethnic groups and immigration===
{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Ethnic groups in Qym ({{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}})|label1=[[Qymish people|Qymish]]|value1=83.7|color1=#dde5b6|label2=[[Rumykian people|Rumykian]]|value2=3.4|color2=#90a955|label3=[[Sunamese people|Sunamese]]|value3=2.7|color3=#4f772d|label4=[[Emadinian people|Emadinian]]|value4=2.2|color4=#31572c|label5=[[Ethnic groups in Qym|Others]]|value5=8.0|color5=#132a13}}{{Main|Ethnic groups of Qym|Immigration to Qym}}

[[Qymish people]] are the largest ethnic group in Qym, at 135,894,969 people or 83.7% of the total population as of the {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} census. Qym is one of the [[Meran]]ian countries with the highest number of registered minorities. With a population of 5,520,226 people, [[Rumykian people|Rumykians]] remain the largest ethnic minority, concentrated predominantly in the [[March of Rumyk]] and representing 3.4% of the country's population (57.3% in the March of Rumyk). [[Sunamese people]], numbering 4,383,709 or 2.7% of the total population, are the second largest minority, mainly concentrated in the [[Duchy of Sunam]]. [[Emadinian people|Emadinians]] form the third largest minority at 3,571,911 people or 2.2% of the country's population. They mainly live in the [[March of Emadin]]. Other registered minority groups include [[Tamodia]]ns (2,273,034 – 1.4%), [[Gosaria]]s (1,461,236 – 0.9%), [[Imendia]]ns (1,136,517 – 0.7%), [[Qarhõnian people|Qarhõnians]] (649,438 – 0.4%) and [[Litagẽyan people|Litagẽyans]] (487,079 – 0.3%). Among the significant non-[[Meran]]ian immigrant minorities are the [[PAHARIAN GROUP 1]] (1.3 million ~ 0.8%), [[PAHARIAN GROUP 2]] (1.1 million ~ 0.7%), [[DAROCHIAN GROUP 1]] (1 million ~ 0.6%), [[DAROCHIAN GROUP 2]] (800,000 ~ 0.5%) and [[VISALIAN GROUP]] (650,000 ~ 0.4%). 

[[Immigration to Qym]] has historically been from neighboring countries in [[Meran]], particularly other [[Sukatic peoples]] or [[Scildism|Scildists]] at large. Starting from the early {{abbr|2390s|1985.5 to 1993.8 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, Qym began to attract substantial flows of immigrants from other continents, particularly [[Pahari]] and [[Daroch]]. In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qymish citizens were subdivided as follow based on continent-of-birth: [[Meran]] (95.6%), [[Pahari]] (2.1%), [[Daroch]] (1.6%), and [[Visala]] (0.7%). As of that same year's census, 8.44 million people or 5.2% of Qymish residents were foreign born. These exclude illegal immigrants, whose numbers are thought to be in the 1–2 million range. The distribution of immigrants in uneven throughout Qym, with major cities hosting the bulk of foreign-born residents.

===Language===
{{Main|Qymish language}}

The official language of Qym is [[Qymish language|Qymish]], a [[Sukatic languages|Sukatic language]]. It is spoken by 139.8 million people (86.36% of the population) as a native language and 22.07 million (13.64% of the population) as a second language. Outside of Qym, it is also spoken in neighboring countries in [[Meran]] and by [[Qymish diaspora]] communities throughout [[Basis]]. The [[Royal academy of the Qymish language]] regulates the use of the language, which comprises 37 recognized dialects. The standard dialect taught in schools throughout Qym is based on the middle and upper class dialect of the [[Duchy of Zhol]], spoken by the [[House of Zhifol|royal family]] and mainly heard on television. 

Five regional languages native to Qym are recognized by Qymish law, namely [[Emadinian language|Emadinian]], [[Litagẽyan language|Litagẽyan]], [[Qarhõnian language|Qarhõnian]], [[Rumykian language|Rumykian]], and [[Sunamese language|Sunamese]]. All these languages are in official use in administrative divisions (duchies, counties, marches, baronies and municipalities) where the ethnic minority exceeds 25% of the total population, based on the [[Regional Languages Royal Decree of 2353]]. [[Emadinian language|Emadinian]] (official in the [[March of Emadin]]) and [[Sunamese language|Sunamese]] (official in the [[Duchy of Sunam]]) are closely related to [[Qymish language|Qymish]], being both [[North Sukatic languages]]. While in the same language family, [[Rumykian language|Rumykian]] (official in the [[March of Rumyk]]) is more distantly related, being classified as [[South Sukatic languages|South Sukatic]]. Those three minority languages, along with Qymish, are also written using the [[Sukatic script]]. Meanwhile, [[Litagẽyan language|Litagẽyan]] (official in the [[March of Litagẽ]]) and [[Qarhõnian language|Qarhõnian]] (official in the [[March of Qarhõ]]) are both [[North Ottelic language]]s and use the [[Ottelian script]]. 

===Religion===
{{Main|Religion in Qym}}[[File:Man and Woman at Dia De Los Muertos (49275956137).jpg|250px|thumb|left|A young [[Scildism|Scildist]] couple during the [[Festival of the Dead]] in {{abbr|2431|2019 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}.]]

Freedom of religion is guaranteed under the [[Fundamental Law of Qym]], but [[Scildism]] remains the country's official religion. According to the {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} census, 93.2% of the population of Qym follows [[Scildism]], 3.7% [[Sunneism]] or [[Mōnaïsm]], 1.5% [[Manigism]] and 0.7% [[Ānism]] ([[One Orthodox Church]]). The remaining 0.9% refused to answer. [[wikipedia:Irreligion|Irreligion]] and [[wikipedia:atheism|atheism]] were not an option on the census, and many Scildists consider themselves irreligious, or at the very least [[wikipedia:Antitheism|antitheist]]. Most practitioners of non-Scildist religions are immigrants. 

[[Sunneism]] is well established, with striving communities, and faces very little stigma compared to other religions. Sociologists explain that this is likely due to the fact the two Sunneist gods, [[Sunne (god)|Sunne]] and [[Mōna (goddess)|Mōna]], are visible physical entities in the material world. There have been attempts by certain Sunneist converts to syncretize the religion with Scildism, particularly during the {{abbr|2370s|1968.8 to 1977.1 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, but their efforts were mainly fruitless. 

[[Manigism]] has been brought to Qym through immigrants in the last fifty years. Practitioners are usually seen with suspicion and there have been attacks on various Manigist communities in Qym in recent decades. The most serious of these was a [[wikipedia:pogrom|mob attack]] in [[Nagur]], on DAY MONTH {{abbr|2405|1998 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} targeting mainly [[Daroch]]ian immigrants. The attack became known as the [[2405 Nagur riots]] and was condemned by [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]]. 

[[Ānism]] is tolerated through the [[One Orthodox Church]], which has legal status, but Ānists face significant stigma in everyday life. Due to its association with [[transhumanism]], the [[One Church of Man]] is banned, as well as most other unofficial Ānist churches.<br><br>

===Health===
[[File:CHUM depuis l'avenue Viger.jpg|250px|thumb|right|A modern red hospital in [[Zhol]].]]{{Main|Healthcare in Qym}}

Qym's universal healthcare system is one of the oldest in Basis, dating back to {{abbr|2210|1836 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. It has however been modernized and changed numerous times over the last two centuries. Since {{abbr|2366|1965.5 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym has used a [[wikipedia:two-tier healthcare|two-tier system]], where a public network of universal [[green hospital]]s accepts all patients, with a guaranteed list of services, while a parallel network of for-profit but also government-owned [[red hospital]]s provides services not available in green hospitals or available with extra amenities such as private rooms, shorter waiting lists, personalized care and also cosmetic surgeries. As a baseline, Qym uses a government-funded universal health insurance. Optional additional supplementary insurance is available for purchase for elective coverage, such as for plastic surgery or for extra amenities in hospitals. 

Only Qymish citizens or foreign permanent residents can use green hospitals, but during emergencies or when the identity of a patient is unknown, first responders will bring the patient to the nearest hospital, which will often be a green one. In such a case, the patient will be treated as normal, but they will be charged red hospitalization costs and if possible eventually transferred to a red hospital. This system was put in place in the 1960s to deal with increased hospitalizations of foreigners due to an increase in tourism. Eventually, this led to [[wikipedia:medical tourism|medical tourism]] as well, due to Qym's high-quality level of medical care and ease of access with the two-tier system. Procedures not available in other countries are also in demand by foreign patients, notably abortions, but also cosmetic interventions. Qym also has a large network of smaller green and red clinics that complement the hospital networks. They are more common in rural areas where hospitals are scarce, and generally offer a lot less services. Healthcare professionals working in either red or green institutions are considered civil servants and their work conditions are similar overall as Qym's "medical private sector" is actually state-owned. Physicians and nurses in green hospitals and clinics are better paid but tend to work longer hours, while those in red institutions have lower salaries, but slightly less hours. 

Vaccination in Qym is mandatory for children and adults alike. Under Qymish law, there is no recognition of an individual's right to their body when it comes to public health. Anti-vaccination activism is prohibited and subject to fines and imprisonment as part of the country's misinformation laws, while repeated offenses can lead to internment into mental institutions or life in prison. Knowingly spreading diseases, such as HIV, is deemed equivalent to murder.

===Education===
[[File:110910032-TUM.jpg|250px|thumb|right|A [[wikipedia:research reactor|research reactor]] of the [[University of Patsalys]].]]{{Main|Education in Qym}}

Qym has an adult literacy rate of 100%, mainly thanks to its universal compulsory education system. The country has free compulsory education for a duration of {{abbr|13 years|11 years (Earth Equivalent)}}, exclusively for children aged {{abbr|7|5.8 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}. Like most countries, the Qymish education system is divided into three institutional networks: primary (ages {{abbr|7|5.8 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|13|10.8 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}), secondary (ages {{abbr|14|11.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|19|15.8 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}), and tertiary (ages {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}+). At the tertiary level, students must attend a [[College education in Qym|college]] and graduate before going to university. College education serves as a bridge between compulsory schooling and university education, where they can specialize in a number of different technical or pre-university fields. College typically takes {{abbr|two years|1.66 years (Earth Equivalent)}} to complete for pre-university and {{abbr|three years|2.49 years (Earth Equivalent)}} for most vocational or technical diplomas, and students are usually between {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} and {{abbr|22|18.3 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}. There are royal colleges, which are public institutions fully funded by the government, with only service fees required of students, and there are private colleges, which are more competitive and require substantial tuition fees. 

Students typical start university when they are {{abbr|22|18.3 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}. Universities in Qym are all private institutions who receive funds from tuition, private donors and government subsidies. Moreover, the [[House of Zhifol|Qymish royal family]], corporations, and NGOs provide scholarships to help promising Qymish students of lower income backgrounds to be able to attend university. According to a [[Basis Forum]] report in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym is the 3rd leading destination for international study. The five top-ranking universities are the [[University of Zhol]], the [[University of Nagur]], [[Darnak University]] (Zhol), the [[University of Patsalys]] and the [[University of Vyshir]] (Zhol). 

Students in secondary schools typically learn a regional language other than Qymish for three of the six years, as well as one foreign language for the remaining three years. In {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, the fractions of students studying foreign languages in Qymish schools included: [[TBD]] – 24.3%, [[TBD]] – 22.1%, [[TBD]] – 16.9%, [[TBD]] – 9.2%, [[TBD]] – 8.6%, [[TBD]] – 7.2%, [[TBD]] – 6.1%, [[TBD]] – 4.3%, [[TBD]] – 0.6%.

